Great to see this kind of information out there. 

@LeRobotHF: "Releasing the Unfolding Robotics blog! Time to unfold robotics: we trained a robot to fold clothes using 8 bimanual setups, 100+ hours of demonstrations, and 5k+ GPU hours. Flashy robot demos are everywhere. But you rarely see the real story: the data, the failures, the engineering. We’re sharing everything: code, data, and details in the blog." 

The linked Hugging Face Space provides access to the full article on teaching a bimanual robot to fold T-shirts, along with a demo video and an interactive table of contents.

The team that co-invented VLAs just abandoned them. This has several implications for robotics teams: if you have enough data, you don't need to inherit someone else's foundation model; starting from scratch can be more effective. The VLA approach existed due to a lack of robotics data, but that assumption is rapidly changing. This shift puts pressure on robotics companies still fine-tuning off-the-shelf VLMs, as starting from scratch raises the cost of entry.

Goals should take precedence over methods; instead of choosing between VLA or world models, focus on how far you can go and remove constraints one by one.

@peteflorence: "In GEN-1, approximately 99% of the parameters are trained from scratch. Previously, this might be considered wild. For Generalist, it’s a deliberate choice. It follows our strong conviction — pursued."

Between this and other recent open-source datasets and work, it feels like there has never been a better time to invest in training robot foundation models. The AGIBOT WORLD 2026 dataset, released by the Chinese robotics company @AGIBOTofficial, systematically covers key embodied AI research directions and is built entirely from real-world environments like commercial spaces and homes.

Collected using AGIBOT G2 robots, the dataset provides structured, accurately annotated, high-quality data, including multiple data types such as RGB(D) cameras, tactile sensors, and LiDAR. It covers tasks like fine-grained manipulation, spatial navigation, and multi-agent collaboration, and includes error-recovery trajectories to teach models how to handle mistakes. Making it open-source accelerates embodied AI research by providing researchers access to high-quality real-world robot data at scale.

@lukas_m_ziegler: "🔥 JUST IN: Open-source robotics dataset from 100% real-world scenarios! 🤯"

Great read! I couldn't agree more with the goal-driven mindset instead of picking labels (e.g., what is a VLA, world model, etc.). It's always intriguing to think about what if robot data is no longer a constraint, and it’s exciting to see how it's being pushed forward by @peteflorence, @andyzengineer, and folks at @GeneralistAI.

To me, the most intriguing question is not whether scaling data just 'solves robotics,' but that it enables us to ask very different questions and enhance our collective understanding of the shared challenge.

@peteflorence: "In GEN-1, approximately 99% of the parameters are trained from scratch. Previously, this might be considered wild. For Generalist, it’s a deliberate choice. It follows our strong conviction — pursued."

The term 'world model' or 'world action model' can be frustrating because it means different things to different people, and those interpretations have vastly different strengths and weaknesses.

@natashamalpani explains that while @ylecun, @nvidia, and @drfeifei are all building world models, they are not creating the same thing. LeCun is focused on cognitive architecture, which builds causal models of reality and plans within them. NVIDIA is developing simulation infrastructure for physics-based environments that train and evaluate physical AI systems at scale. Fei-Fei Li is working on spatial intelligence, aiming to create systems that understand and reason about physical space. 

Bundling these approaches under one term inflates hype and obscures where real value accumulates. The simulation moat is currently strong due to the scarcity of native physical interaction data, but as robotics companies gather real interaction data, that scarcity may diminish. The spatial intelligence approach is more grounded, providing machines with accurate models of physical space, while the cognitive architecture approach is the most ambitious and requires components that do not yet exist in current systems.

