Teams from various universities, including Peking University and TUM, collaborated on the TienKung platform, demonstrating an open ecosystem with shared hardware and different solutions. Just a day before, TienKung Ultra completed the Robot Warrior Challenge, navigating obstacle tasks modeled on real-world rescue operations. The focus is shifting from speed to autonomy and the ability of these systems to handle real environments.

@XRoboHub: "50:26. Let that sink in — a humanoid just won the Beijing half marathon at a pace faster than the human world record. The winner, 'Lightning' from the Monkey King team, ran a sub-51 minute race, while the human record sits at 56:42. This one is built for speed — around 169 cm, mech-style design, optimized for aerodynamics and burst power. There are two versions: remote-controlled and fully autonomous. And yes, the autonomous one can actually navigate and hold that pace. At this point, the conversation really changes. It’s no longer about whether robots can run long distance — it’s about how fast they can push past human limits."

